    {
      "id": "j302-async-jshell",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "//@ anchor: jshell-mode-async-composition-with-c\n//@ description: 🔵 JShell Mode — async composition with CompletableFuture\n// --- JShell mode: composing work that has not finished yet ---\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\n\n// A CompletableFuture<String> is a promise of a String that may not exist yet.\n// supplyAsync hands the work to the common ForkJoinPool and returns\n// immediately, so this method does not block its caller.\nCompletableFuture<String> fetchUser(int id) {\n    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {\n        // In real code this would be an HTTP call or a database query.\n        return \"User-\" + id;\n    });\n}\n\nCompletableFuture<String> fetchEmail(String user) {\n    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> user + \"@example.com\");\n}\n\n// thenCompose vs thenApply is the distinction worth learning:\n//   thenApply(f)   - f returns a plain value        -> CompletableFuture<T>\n//   thenCompose(f) - f returns ANOTHER future       -> flattened, not nested\n// Using thenApply here would give CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<String>>.\nvar result = fetchUser(42)\n    .thenCompose(user -> fetchEmail(user))\n    .thenApply(email -> \"Contact: \" + email);\n\n// get() is where the calling thread finally waits. Everything above merely\n// wired up the chain; nothing blocked until this line.\nSystem.out.println(result.get());\n\n// thenCombine joins two INDEPENDENT futures. They run concurrently and the\n// combiner is called once both have completed - total time is the slower of\n// the two, not their sum.\nvar f1 = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> \"Hello\");\nvar f2 = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> \"World\");\nvar combined = f1.thenCombine(f2, (a, b) -> a + \", \" + b + \"!\");\nSystem.out.println(combined.get());\n\n// A failed future carries the exception forward instead of throwing on the\n// spot. exceptionally() is the recovery stage: it is skipped entirely when\n// nothing went wrong, and substitutes a value when something did.\nvar failing = CompletableFuture.<String>failedFuture(new RuntimeException(\"oops\"))\n    .exceptionally(ex -> \"Recovered: \" + ex.getMessage());\nSystem.out.println(failing.get());",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": null,
      "error": "",
      "anchor": "jshell-mode-async-composition-with-c",
      "dependsOn": [],
